  "question": {
    "text": "A symmetrical form of the line of intersection of the planes $x=a y+b$ and $z=c y+d$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "I3P5d",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{x-b}{a}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z-d}{c}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "HwgTM",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{x-b-a}{a}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z-d-c}{c}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "N2wmQ",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{\\mathrm{x}-\\mathrm{a}}{\\mathrm{b}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{y}-0}{1}=\\frac{\\mathrm{z}-\\mathrm{c}}{\\mathrm{d}}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "uOh8A",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{\\mathrm{x}-\\mathrm{b}-\\mathrm{a}}{\\mathrm{b}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{y}-1}{0}=\\frac{\\mathrm{z}-\\mathrm{d}-\\mathrm{c}}{\\mathrm{d}}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given two planes:\n$$\nx-a y-b=0 \\text { and } c y-z+d=0\n$$\nLet, $l, m, n$ be the direction ratio of the required line. Since the required line is perpendicular to normal of both the plane, therefore $l-a m=0$ and $c m-n=0$ $\\Rightarrow l-a m+0 . n=0$ and $0 . l+c m-n=0$\n$$\n\\therefore \\quad \\frac{l}{a-0}=\\frac{m}{0+1}=\\frac{n}{c-0}\n$$\nHence, d.R of the required line are $a, 1$, $c$.\nHence, options (c) and (d) are rejected. Now, the point $(a+b, 1, c+d)$ satisfy the equation of the two given planes.\n$\\therefore$ Option (b) is correct.",
    "image": null
  },
